+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits Resolve as Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Workers collect visits offline for up to 48 hours and sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. When two edits derive from the same starting visit state, the record store must decide what the current visit is. Arrival order is not evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative, and the project's central failure to avoid is a correction disappearing without trace (today's spreadsheet imports overwrite corrections without showing which submission was lost).
+
+A visit's scientific fields are related in ways the server cannot fully see. Correcting an observer name and adding a missing species are unrelated edits; revising a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation split across two fields. Field names alone do not distinguish the two cases.
+
+This decision sets the submission metadata tablets must send, the shape of review work, and which visit states may enter the Friday export. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it, so a wrong choice is expensive to reverse once those designs exist.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **Original submissions are never discarded.** Inherited from the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*. Every option must pass.
+- **Capture works offline for 48 hours.** Inherited from the HLD tenet *Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*. Every option must pass; it constrains what a tablet must retain, not which option is chosen.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Scientific integrity of the resolved visit** — **major**. A resolved visit should never contain a combination of fields that no worker submitted and no coordinator reviewed. Derives from the HLD non-goal of automated inference of scientific intent and from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Explainability of exported values** — **major**. A coordinator or partner should be able to trace an exported visit to one submission or one explicit resolution. Derives from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Review volume** — **moderate**. Fewer visits needing a coordinator means more visits reach the Friday export. Two coordinators handle roughly 6,000 visits per season, and the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* makes sustained queue growth a real cost.
+- **Maintenance burden** — **moderate**. Rules that must be kept correct as the survey protocol evolves require someone to own them. Same tenet as above.
+- **Tablet and submission size** — **minor**. Local storage and payload size on shared tablets with intermittent connectivity.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission is a complete visit that names the revision it started from. When a submission's base revision is no longer the visit's latest, the visit holds competing complete revisions. A coordinator resolves them; until then the visit has unresolved review work and is not exportable. Nothing is combined by the system.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name on revision 3 and submits revision 4a. Worker B, offline, adds a missing species to revision 3 and submits 4b. Both are retained intact; the visit shows two competing revisions for a coordinator, even though the edits touch different fields.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Original submissions retained: **passes**. Revisions are the stored unit.
+- Offline capture: **passes**. A tablet needs only the base revision ID.
+- Scientific integrity: **strong**. Every resolved state is a visit a worker submitted or a coordinator explicitly approved.
+- Explainability: **strong**. An exported value traces to one revision and one resolution record.
+- Review volume: **weak**. Unrelated edits to the same visit still create review work.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong**. No field-level rules exist to maintain.
+- Tablet and submission size: **strong**. Tablets keep a revision ID and send the whole visit.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base snapshot and a structured set of field changes. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically and sends overlapping changes to review. Merge rules cover scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries.
+
+**Demonstration.** Under the same scenario, the observer-name correction and the added species combine into one revision without coordinator involvement. A change to a species identification by A and a cover change on that species by B overlap by entry and go to review only if the rules recognize the relationship.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Original submissions retained: **passes**. Original payloads are kept alongside the merged result.
+- Offline capture: **passes**. A tablet keeps a base snapshot per draft.
+- Scientific integrity: **weak**. Disjoint field edits can still be scientifically related, and the rules cannot know every relationship; a combined visit may be one no worker observed.
+- Explainability: **partial**. Each field traces to a contributing submission, but the visit as a whole has no single author and needs field-level provenance.
+- Review volume: **strong**. Unrelated edits combine automatically.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak**. Rules for scalar, list, and species-entry fields must be kept correct as the protocol changes.
+- Tablet and submission size: **partial**. Base snapshots and structured changes are held and sent.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+Both options pass the gates, so the decision turns on the two major criteria against review volume. Whole-visit revisions are strong on scientific integrity and explainability, which derive from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal against inferring scientific intent. Field-aware merge is weak or partial on both. Its advantage, review volume, is a moderate criterion, and the cost of a larger review queue is visible and bounded, while the cost of a silently combined scientific record is neither.
+
+Committing forecloses automatic combination of any edits. Every concurrent edit to a visit, however unrelated, becomes review work, and the Friday export holds those visits back until resolved.
+
+Downstream obligations:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID for each local draft, sends it with each submission, and shows a worker when competing revisions exist for a visit they edited. It does not need base snapshots or structured change sets.
+- **Review-export** provides a coordinator comparison of competing revisions, records the resolution (coordinator identity and contributing submissions), and excludes visits with unresolved revisions from the export set. It sizes its queue and export-eligibility rules around that exclusion.
+
+## Conditions to Revisit
+
+Field-aware merge remains a credible option and should be reconsidered if:
+
+- the unresolved-visit queue routinely prevents coordinators from clearing work before the Friday export, and the editing pattern shows most competing revisions touch plainly unrelated fields; or
+- the survey protocol stabilizes enough that the relationships between fields (for example, species identification and cover estimate) can be stated as a short, owned rule set.
+
+Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that coordinators approve combined records: a later design could present a proposed combination as a coordinator-reviewed candidate revision, which this decision's revision model can carry without change to what tablets send. The observed rate of competing revisions per season is not yet known; it is the evidence that would settle whether to revisit.
